The practice: given a struct with fields, produce a human-readable string of its current state.
The mechanism is serialization — converting an in-memory structured representation to a linear form. This is the upstream category. 

Serialization has several distinct purposes:

    persistence (write to disk, read back)
    wire transmission (network protocols, IPC)
    human inspection — which is what we're doing here

That last slice has a name in well-typed languages: Haskell calls it Show, Rust splits it into Display (user-facing) and Debug (programmer-facing, auto-derivable). C++ has no formalized equivalent — operator<< / to_string is the idiomatic approximation.


The purpose of human-inspection serialization in a running system is observability — the ability to inspect system state. Observability decomposes into: logging (timestamped runtime events), debugging (ad-hoc inspection), tracing (execution flow), metrics (quantitative). to_string is a primitive that enables all of these; it's not itself logging or debugging — it's the representation layer underneath.